nd the  healthy brain especially  also the disorders of the brain dr  calhoun creates algorithms which seek to  map the dynamic networks of the brain  structure  um and the involvement of genetics and  how these are affected  um while being stimulated by various  tasks or individuals with mental  illnesses  dr calhoun is an author on more than 650  peer-reviewed journal articles  over 750 technical reports abstracts and  conference proceedings  he served in many leadership roles in  the field of neuroimaging and data  science  he is a fellow of the um uh institute of  electrical  uh and electronic engineers a past chair  of the organization for human brain  mapping  amongst other accolades uh he's been  awarded over a million dollars  or sorry a hundred million dollars and  counting in federal research grants  to study the topics of of neuroimaging  related research  and his talk today is entitled  brain-based biomarkers a focus on the  heterogeneous  enhancing sensitivity to brain disorders  and change as always  we are streaming this lecture live and  for recording by youtube and if you are  watching on youtube thank you so much  for joining us  also our specially selected 2020  uh 2020 2021 biomedical data science  innovation lab participants  are encouraged to submit any questions  for dr calhoun by the chat feature in  their zoom sessions i'll synthesize  these questions and ask them on your  behalf  at the end of the lecture and with that  welcome vince we are so excited to hear  about your talk today thanks jack it's a  pleasure to be here  um what i am going to be doing today is  talking about some of our work in trying  to move towards brain-based biomarkers  um but in particular with a a focus on  heterogeneity um you know and how do we  use that  in in order to enhance our sensitivity  um so i'll go through kind of some some  uh preliminary work  um and motivation and then i'll talk  about sort of a number of different  ways in which we've tried to tackle this  issue  um so i think um  when we're trying to develop you know  neuroimaging  biomarkers of brain disorders or health  um there's often this sort of back and  forth between a focus on sort of  individual changes and you know  canonical  changes like do we can we find something  that's sort of representative right of a  group or a  a set of of individuals and that's kind  of a  circle or a cycle and and if you look at  the field you see studies go back and  forth between  group differences individual prediction  individualized change  than trying to develop more general  generalizable  information so um i think this is really  kind of an important  cycle and that's a little bit what i'll  be touching on today  um there have been sort of a huge  increase in  recent years in studies focused on  trying to get at  sort of prediction right accuracy  classification etc  this is a review from a number of years  ago where we started to look at this and  you can see  you know sort of some of the accuracies  here across various  uh brain disorders uh there's a pretty  big spread  um there's also you know a trend of  higher accuracies with smaller end data  sets which which probably gets at some  bias  but there's also you know quite a bit of  evidence that  brain imaging can be useful and accurate  at predicting various aspects  um that may potentially be important for  uh clinical translation  um and we can predict you know not only  do  doing sort of binary classification of  you know group a versus group b but you  can also look at sort of regression or  predicting continuous continuous  measures like symptoms or cognitive  scores  etc and this is sort of a more recent  review where we kind of looked at this  and a couple of the take homes we found  here  when you incorporate multiple imaging  modalities you tend to get higher  accuracies uh than unimodal you can kind  of see the distribution here  and the other is that you know if you're  looking at a patient group 